architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "sheni_10"
  title: A range whose ends may be unknown is one type, and the standard chooses its fallback
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-08-29"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  open-questions:
    - The fourth axis, anchoring, has no type. Sheni's `timestamp` is RFC 3339 and requires an offset, so a local wall-clock reading that travels with the reader -- "07:00, wherever I am" -- cannot be written, and neither can a recurring routine built on one. It is a different standard from the other three axes and nothing downstream is blocked on it, so it is recorded as a gap rather than designed here.
  context: |
    ADR sheni_06 deferred EDTF's interval to a sibling type and said why: a
    range is a pair and a date is a point, and one type that is sometimes each
    makes every comparison branch. It named the sibling `soft_date_range` and
    left it for its own record. This is that record.

    It closes the third of four axes that a planning date needs, which are
    worth keeping apart because only three of them are the same standard.
    Precision -- day, month, quarter, year -- and certainty -- uncertain,
    approximate -- are ADR sheni_06's, and both are EDTF. Range is this
    record, and it is EDTF too. Anchoring -- whether a time is an instant
    everyone agrees on or a local wall-clock reading that travels with the
    reader -- is RFC 5545 rather than EDTF, applies to times rather than
    dates, and stays open.

    The open-ended deadline was one of the cases that motivated ADR sheni_06,
    and that record listed leaving it unexpressible as a cost of deferring.
    Nothing else in the crate can say "from August until whenever".
  decision: |
    A new simple type, `soft_date_range`, delegating to `edtf-core` as
    `soft_date` does, accepting the `Edtf::Interval` shape and no other.

    An endpoint is a date at any precision `soft_date` accepts, or `..` for
    open, or empty for unknown. The two are different claims and EDTF keeps
    them apart: open says the range genuinely has no end, and unknown says it
    has one that nobody has recorded. A dated endpoint beside `..` reads as on
    or before, or on or after, which is how "by the end of the year" is
    written. The separator is `/`, which SYON carries without comment.

    The fallback is `XXXX/XXXX`, and the standard chose it rather than this
    record. The obvious spelling for a range that is entirely unknown is `/`,
    or `../..`, and `edtf-core` rejects both: an interval needs at least one
    dated endpoint. `XXXX/XXXX` is accepted, because a fully unspecified date
    is dated -- it is a date that specifies nothing, which is precisely the
    fallback ADR sheni_06 gave `soft_date`. So the fallback for the range is
    the fallback for the point at both ends, arrived at by composition and
    confirmed by the delegate rather than invented here.

    That is worth stating as the general habit it is. Where a standard already
    answers a question, the answer is looked up rather than decided, and where
    the standard refuses something the refusal is evidence and not an
    obstacle. This crate has now taken the same route three times: `soft_date`
    excluding sets because SYON cannot carry them, ADR sheni_09 refusing
    `soft_string` because `string` has no text left over, and this fallback.

    Errors share the band ADR sheni_06 opened, because the two types fail in
    the same three ways. 234 is text that is not EDTF at all. 235 is a set,
    which is unwritable in SYON rather than merely unsupported. 236 is
    well-formed EDTF of the wrong shape for the declared type, and the message
    names the type the author wanted -- an interval read at `soft_date` says
    to declare `soft_date_range`, a date read at `soft_date_range` says to
    declare `soft_date`, and a date with a time of day says to declare
    `timestamp`. Right family, wrong member is one problem and takes one code;
    the message carries the specifics, as ADR syon_08 has it.

    One caveat belongs on the record because it is invisible until it bites.
    EDTF's uncertainty marker is `?`, which both implementations carry happily
    in a value, and which the Go implementation rejects at the start of a
    *key* as YAML's complex-key indicator. A soft date used as a map key and
    written `?2026-06` would therefore parse in Rust and fail in Go. It is
    narrow: EDTF's canonical form writes the qualifier last, so the value
    normalises to `2026-06?` and reads back portably. Recorded rather than
    fixed, since the fix belongs to whoever puts a soft date in a map key.

    One shape is deliberately not this type, and it is wanted. A range is two
    endpoints. "Two hours, somewhere between Monday and Friday" is a duration
    and a window, which is a different shape and not an interval -- the
    endpoints bound where the work may fall, and the duration says how much of
    it there is. That is a struct composing `duration` with this type, it
    belongs in group 3, and it is committed rather than conditional: it will
    be built once group 3 exists, and it is the first curated struct with a
    reason to exist beyond demonstrating that structs work.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - The open-ended deadline becomes expressible, which ADR sheni_06 listed as a cost of deferring and is now paid back.
      - Open and unknown stay distinct, so "runs indefinitely" and "nobody recorded an end" are different values rather than one convention.
      - The fallback is composed from `soft_date`'s and confirmed by the delegate, so an optional range field needs no sentinel and nothing was invented to get one.
      - Sharing the error band with `soft_date` keeps one vocabulary for two types that fail identically, and 236 tells an author which of the two they wanted.
      - Both types read back canonically, so two applications exchanging a range agree on its text without negotiating anything.
      - The four axes are now written down, so the one that is not EDTF is not mistaken for one that is.
    negative:
      - A third date type in group 2, and the choice between `date`, `soft_date` and `soft_date_range` is one more thing a schema author has to get right.
      - EDTF's interval syntax is unfamiliar, and the difference between `2026-08/..` and `2026-08/` is one character carrying a real distinction.
      - The `?`-as-a-map-key divergence is recorded and not fixed, so it can still surprise someone who writes a soft date as a key before it normalises.
      - `XXXX/XXXX` is a strange thing to read in a document, and its being the standard's choice rather than ours does not make it obvious.
      - The fourth axis stays open, so a floating or local-time range is still unexpressible and this record explains why without solving it.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: One type covering both the point and the range
      reason: Rejected in ADR sheni_06 and unchanged here. A value that is sometimes a point and sometimes a pair makes every comparison branch on which it is.
    -
      name: A struct holding two `soft_date` fields, in group 3
      reason: It cannot express an open end, since `..` is not a date and has no `soft_date` to hold it. It would also discard the canonical text form, which is the thing that makes the value portable between applications.
    -
      name: Accept `/` or `../..` for a wholly unknown range, extending the standard
      reason: The standard refuses them deliberately -- an interval needs a dated endpoint -- and text this crate accepts that EDTF rejects is not EDTF. `XXXX/XXXX` says the same thing and is portable.
    -
      name: Give `soft_date_range` no fallback, so a range field is always required
      reason: An unscheduled task with no window yet is the ordinary case in the tools this type exists for, and ADR sheni_03 makes a fallback the only route to an optional field.
    -
      name: A separate error band for ranges
      reason: The two types fail in the same three ways at the same delegate. Two vocabularies for one set of failures would make a caller match twice to ask one question.
    -
      name: Model a duration floating inside a window here
      reason: It is a duration and a window rather than a pair of endpoints, so it is a different shape wearing a similar name. It composes this type with `duration`, and it is deferred to group 3 rather than declined -- see the decision, which commits to building it there.
